To compile a directory (scan the source files):
  If the compiler's abort flag is set, exit.
  Write in strong green "                            [OK]" then the CRLF string to Stdout.
  Write in strong blue " [03/16]" to StdOut.
  Write "  Scanning source files…" to Stdout.
  Start the scanning timer.
  Scan the source files.
  Stop the scanning timer.

To scan some source files:
  [Loop.]
    If the compiler's abort flag is set, exit.
    Get a source file from the source files.
    If the source file is nil, exit.
    Scan the source file.
  Repeat.

To scan a source file:
  If the source file is nil, exit.
  If the compiler's abort flag is set, exit.
  Slap a rider on the source file's buffer.
  Move the rider (compiler rules).
  Loop.
    If the compiler's abort flag is set, exit.
    If the rider's token substring is blank, exit.
    If the rider's token substring is the start of any type,
      Scan a type given the rider;
      Repeat.
    If the rider's token substring is the start of any global,
      Scan a global given the rider;
      Repeat.
    If the rider's token substring is the start of any routine,
      Scan a routine given the rider;
      Repeat.
    Abort with "Error #4. Expected the start of a type definition, global variable declaration, or routine declaration, but found '" then the rider's token substring then "'." then the CRLF string then "Note: this is often caused by an unsupported character encoding or incorrect line endings." and the rider.
  Repeat.

To move a rider (compiler rules):
  [Loop.]
    If the compiler's abort flag is set,
      Clear the rider's token substring;
      Exit.
    Move the rider returning an error string (code rules).
    If the error string is not blank,
      Abort with the error string and the rider's token substring's first byte pointer;
      Exit.
    If the rider's token substring is blank, exit.
    If the rider's token substring's first byte pointer's target is noise, repeat.
    If the rider's token substring's first byte pointer's target is the backslash byte, repeat.
    If the rider's token substring's first byte pointer's target is the left-bracket byte, repeat.

To move a rider returning an error string (code rules):
  Clear the error string.
  Move the rider (code rules).
  If the rider's token substring is blank, exit.
  If the rider's token substring's first byte pointer's target is the left-bracket byte,
    Move the rider returning the error string (code rules - validate remark);
    Exit.
  If the rider's token substring's first byte pointer's target is the double-quote byte,
    Move the rider returning the error string (code rules - validate string);
    Exit.
  If the rider's token substring's first byte pointer's target is the left-paren byte,
    Move the rider returning the error string (code rules - validate qualifier);
    Exit.

To move a rider (code rules):
  Position the rider's token on the rider's source.
  If the rider's source substring is blank, exit.
  If the rider's source substring's first byte pointer's target is noise,
    Move the rider (code rules - noise);
    Exit.
  If the rider's source substring's first byte pointer's target is the backslash byte,
    Move the rider (code rules - comment);
    Exit.
  If the rider's source substring's first byte pointer's target is the left-bracket byte,
    Move the rider (code rules - remark);
    Exit.
  If the rider's source substring's first byte pointer's target is the double-quote byte,
    Move the rider (code rules - string);
    Exit.
  If the rider's source substring's first byte pointer's target is the left-paren byte,
    Move the rider (code rules - qualifier);
    Exit.
  If the rider's source substring's first byte pointer's target is any mark,
    Move the rider (code rules - mark);
    Exit.
  If the rider is on any possessive,
    Move the rider (code rules - possessive);
    Exit.
  Move the rider (code rules - glom).

To move a rider (code rules - noise):
  [Loop.]
    Bump the rider.
    If the rider's source substring is blank, exit.
    If the rider's source substring's first byte pointer's target is noise, repeat.

To move a rider (code rules - comment):
  [Loop.]
    Bump the rider.
    If the rider's source substring is blank, exit.
    If the rider's source substring's first byte pointer's target is not the return byte, repeat.

To move a rider (code rules - remark):
  [Loop.]
    If the rider's source substring is blank, exit.
    If the rider's source substring's first byte pointer's target is the return byte, break.
    If the rider's source substring's first byte pointer's target is the left-bracket byte,
      Add 1 to a count.
    If the rider's source substring's first byte pointer's target is the right-bracket byte,
      Subtract 1 from the count.
    Bump the rider.
    If the count is 0, break.
  Repeat.

To move a rider (code rules - string):
  [Loop.]
    Bump the rider.
    If the rider's source substring is blank, exit.
    If the rider's source substring's first byte pointer's target is the return byte, exit.
    If the rider is on any nested double-quote,
      Bump the rider;
      Repeat.
    If the rider's source substring's first byte pointer's target is the double-quote byte,
      Bump the rider;
      Exit.
  Repeat.

To decide if a rider is on any nested double-quote:
  If the rider's source substring starts with """""", say yes.
  Say no.

To move a rider (code rules - qualifier):
  [Loop.]
    If the rider's source substring is blank, exit.
    If the rider's source substring's first byte pointer's target is the return byte, break.
    If the rider's source substring's first byte pointer's target is the left-paren byte,
      Add 1 to a number called count.
    If the rider's source substring's first byte pointer's target is the right-paren byte,
      Subtract 1 from the count.
    Bump the rider.
    If the count is 0, break.
  Repeat.

To decide if a byte is any mark:
  If the byte is the period byte, say yes.
  If the byte is the comma byte, say yes.
  If the byte is the semi-colon byte, say yes.
  If the byte is the colon byte, say yes.
  If the byte is the exclamation  byte, say yes.
  If the byte is the question-mark byte, say yes.
  Say no.

To move a rider (code rules - mark):
  Bump the rider.

To move a rider (code rules - possessive):
  Bump the rider.
  If the rider's source substring is blank, exit.
  If the rider's source substring starts with "s",
    Bump the rider.

To move a rider (code rules - glom):
  [Loop.]
    Bump the rider.
    If the rider's source substring is blank, exit.
    If the rider is on any possessive, exit.
    If the rider's source substring's first byte pointer's target is any glom byte, repeat.

To decide if a rider is on any possessive:
  If the rider's source substring is blank, say no.
  If the rider's source substring's first byte pointer's target is not the single-quote byte, say no.
  If the rider is on any possessive (type 1 - single-quote, s, non-glom-byte), say yes.
  If the rider is on any possessive (type 2 - preceding s, single-quote, non-glom-byte), say yes.
  Say no.

To decide if a rider is on any possessive (type 1 - single-quote, s, non-glom-byte):
  If the rider's source substring does not start with "'s", say no.
  Privatize the rider.
  Add 2 to the rider's source substring's first byte pointer.
  If the rider's source substring is blank, say yes.
  If the rider's source substring's first byte pointer's target is not any glom byte, say yes.
  Say no.

To decide if a byte is any glom byte:
  If the byte is any letter, say yes.
  If the byte is any digit, say yes.
  If the byte is the tilde byte, say yes.
  If the byte is the at-sign byte, say yes.
  If the byte is the number-sign byte, say yes.
  If the byte is the percent-sign byte, say yes.
  If the byte is the ampersand byte, say yes.
  If the byte is the underscore byte, say yes.
  If the byte is the single-quote byte, say yes.
  If the byte is the dash byte, say yes.
  If the byte is the cross byte, say yes.
  If the byte is the slash byte, say yes.
  Say no.

To decide if a rider is on any possessive (type 2 - preceding s, single-quote, non-glom-byte):
  Privatize the rider.
  If the rider's source substring's first byte pointer is the rider's original's first byte pointer, say no.
  Subtract 1 from the rider's source substring's first byte pointer.
  If the rider's source substring's first byte pointer's target is not the little-s byte or the big-s byte, say no.
  Add 2 to the rider's source substring's first byte pointer.
  If the rider's source substring is blank, say yes.
  If the rider's source substring's first byte pointer's target is not any glom byte, say yes.
  Say no.

To move a rider returning an error string (code rules - validate remark):
  If the rider's token's length is less than 2,
    Put "Error #5. Unterminated block comment. Remarks need to end with ']'." into the error string;
    Exit.
  If the rider's token's last's target is not the right-bracket byte,
    Put "Error #6. Unterminated block comment. Remarks need to end with ']'." into the error string;
    Exit.

To move a rider returning an error string (code rules - validate string):
  If the rider's token's length is less than 2,
    Put "Error #7. Unterminated string literal. Strings literals need to end with '""'. Add the missing closing quote." into the error string;
    Exit.
  If the rider's token's last's target is not the double-quote byte,
    Put "Error #8. Unterminated string literal. Strings literals need to end with '""'. Add the missing closing quote." into the error string;
    Exit.

To move a rider returning an error string (code rules - validate qualifier):
  If the rider's token's length is less than 2,
    Put "Error #9. Qualifiers need to end with ')'. Add the missing closing parenthesis." into the error string;
    Exit.
  If the rider's token's last's target is not the right-paren byte,
    Put "Error #10. Qualifiers need to end with ')'. Add the missing closing parenthesis." into the error string;
    Exit.

To decide if a string is the start of any type:
  If the string is "a", say yes.
  If the string is "an", say yes.
  If the string is "some", say yes.
  Say no.

To scan a type given a rider:
  If the compiler's abort flag is set, exit.
  Add the type to the types given the rider's token substring's first byte pointer.
  Move the rider (compiler rules).
  Scan the type's name given the rider.
  Put the type's name into the type's plural name.
  Pluralize the type's plural name.
  If the rider's token substring is "has" or "have",
    Scan the type given the rider (has or have);
    Exit.
  If the rider's token substring is not "is" or "are",
    Abort with "Error #11. Expected an 'is' or 'are' after the type name, but found '" then the rider's token substring then "'." and the rider;
    Exit.
  Move the rider (compiler rules).
  If the rider's token substring is any numeric literal,
    Scan the type given the rider (unit of measure);
    Exit.
  If the rider's token substring is not any indefinite article,
    Abort with "Error #12. Expected an indefinte article before the type definition, but found '" then the rider's token substring then "'." and the rider;
    Exit.
  Move the rider (compiler rules).
  Scan the type's base name given the rider.
  Scan any optional info for the type given the rider.
  If the rider's token substring is not the period byte,
    Abort with "Error #13. Type definitions need to end with a period, found '" then the rider's token substring then "'." and the rider;
    Exit.
  Move the rider (compiler rules).
  Index the type.

To add a type to some types given a locus:
  Create the type.
  Append the type to the types.
  Put the locus into the type's locus.

To scan a name given a rider:
  Clear the name.
  If the compiler's abort flag is set, exit.
  If the rider's token substring is any name starter,
    Extend the name with the rider's token substring;
    Move the rider (compiler rules).
  Loop.
    If the compiler's abort flag is set, exit.
    If the rider's token substring is any name ender, break.
    Extend the name with the rider's token.
    Move the rider (compiler rules).
  Repeat.
  If the name is blank,
    Abort with "Error #14. Expected a type name or variable name before a name ender, but found '" then the rider's token substring then "'." and the rider;
    Exit.

To decide if a string is any name starter: \ compare with name ender
  If the string is any mark, say no.
  If the string is any symbol, say no.
  If the string is any article, say no.
  If the string is any conjunction, say yes.
  If the string is any possessive, say no.
  If the string is any verb, say yes.
  If the string is any operator, say yes.
  If the string is any preposition, say yes.
  If the string is any qualifier, say no.
  If the string is any literal, say no.
  If the string is "called", say yes.
  If the string is "equal", say yes.
  If the string is blank, say no.
  Say yes.

To decide if a byte is any symbol:
  If the byte is the caret byte, say yes.
  If the byte is the bar byte, say yes.
  If the byte is the asterisk byte, say yes.
  If the byte is the cross byte, say yes.
  If the byte is the dash byte, say yes.
  If the byte is the slash byte, say yes.
  If the byte is the left-alligator byte, say yes.
  If the byte is the right-alligator byte, say yes.
  If the byte is the left-brace byte, say yes.
  If the byte is the right-brace byte, say yes.
  If the byte is the equal-sign byte, say yes.
  If the byte is 128, say yes.
  If the byte is 130, say yes.
  If the byte is between 132 and 137, say yes.
  If the byte is 139, say yes.
  If the byte is between 145 and 153, say yes.
  If the byte is 155, say yes.
  If the byte is between 161 and 180, say yes.
  If the byte is between 183 and 191, say yes.
  If the byte is 215, say yes.
  If the byte is 247, say yes.
  Say no.

To decide if a string is any article:
  If the string is any indefinite article, say yes.
  If the string is "the", say yes.
  Say no.

To decide if a string is any conjunction:
  If the string is "and", say yes.
  If the string is "both", say yes.
  If the string is "but", say yes.
  If the string is "either", say yes.
  If the string is "neither", say yes.
  If the string is "nor", say yes.
  If the string is "or", say yes.
  Say no.

To decide if a string is any possessive:
  If the string is "'s", say yes.
  If the string is "'", say yes.
  Say no.

To decide if a string is any verb:
  If the string is "are", say yes. \ are not \ aren't
  If the string is "be", say yes. \
  If the string is "can", say yes. \ cannot \ can't
  If the string is "cannot", say yes.
  If the string is "could", say yes. \ could not \ couldn't
  If the string is "do", say yes. \ do not \ don't
  If the string is "does", say yes. \ does not \ doesn't
  If the string is "is", say yes. \ is not \ isn't
  If the string is "may", say yes. \ may not \ mayn't
  If the string is "should", say yes. \ should not \ shouldn't
  If the string is "was", say yes. \ was not \ wasn't
  If the string is "will", say yes. \ will not \ won't
  If the string is "would", say yes. \ would not \ wouldn't
  If the string is "has", say yes. \ used in type definitions
  If the string is "have", say yes. \ used in type definitions
  If the string ends with "n't", say yes.
  \ trouble makers
  If the string is "begins", say yes. \ does not begin \ doesn't begin
  If the string is "ends", say yes. \ does not end \ doesn't end
  If the string is "looks", say yes. \ does not look \ doesn't look
  If the string is "needs", say yes. \ does not need \ doesn't need
  If the string is "starts", say yes. \ does not start \ doesn't start
  Say no.

To decide if a string is any operator:
  If the string is "plus", say yes.
  If the string is "minus", say yes.
  If the string is "times", say yes.
  If the string is "divided", say yes.
  If the string is "then", say yes.
  Say no.

